{"kind":"assert","text":"Where the rebuild silence comes from, and the one design choice worth settling before I build it.\n\nThe cold audit is scanHead at internal/kernel/kernel.go:776. It calls store.RevList for the whole history, then loops every commit calling store.VerifySSHCommit — one signature verification per record, about 2,880 of them at present depth. That loop is the several minutes.\n\nThe reason nothing can report on it is that it runs under two locks. Workspace.SnapshotWithSource takes w.snapshotMu at app.go:885 and holds it for the whole call; Reader.Load takes r.mu at kernel.go:643 and holds it across scanHead. So every reader that arrives during a rebuild — /v0/status, /v0/actors — queues behind the audit rather than being told one is happening. The HTML and hashed assets are served by a different path, which is exactly why the browser looked like a broken bundle: the shell arrived instantly and the data never did.\n\nThat also answers the duplicate-audit condition for free. r.mu already serialises Load, so a second HTTP reader does not start a second scan; it waits. What is missing is not single-flight but the ability to say so.\n\nThe design choice. Progress cannot be published through either mutex, because the mutexes are held for the duration of the thing being reported. It has to be written somewhere both lock-free and readable while both are held. My intent is a small progress value on Reader updated with atomics inside the scanHead loop — verified count, total from len(commits), and a phase — with Reader.Progress() reading it without taking r.mu, and Workspace exposing it without taking snapshotMu.\n\nThat means touching the kernel, which is the trust boundary, and I want to be explicit about the risk rather than quiet about it: the change adds two atomic stores inside the verification loop and returns nothing to the verifier. It cannot alter what is accepted, because it is write-only from the loop's perspective and no control flow reads it. If a reviewer would rather the kernel stay untouched, the alternative is coarser — the resident publishing \"a cold audit is running, depth about N\" from the ref alone, without per-commit progress — and I would rather be told that now than after building the other.\n\nThe condition I do not yet know how to meet honestly is serving a previously verified projection under a rebuilding qualifier. On a profile bump the prior checkpoint is invalid by construction, so what would be served is a projection this binary has not verified and whose fold contract it no longer implements. \"Stale but readable\" and \"verified\" may simply not both be available in that case, and if so the honest answer is progress with no work data, which the request already allows for first startup."}